Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
i will let's speak now to the armed  forces minister james heavy mr [ __ ] good  to see you this morning and of course  plenty to discuss in terms of those  conversations with the u.s president but  i wonder if we might begin just just  with an update on the state of the  evacuation process how many how many we  perhaps expect to get out over the next  24 hours  indeed so in the last 24-hour period  across eight flights the royal air force  have extracted 1821  people from kabul the flow remains good  coming into the ehc at the barron hotel  and we expect to have a further nine  flights over the next 24 hours  um i wonder if you might confirm uh  reports uh kicking around this morning i  think in the times that the evacuation  program has been has been significantly  expanded last week we were hearing four  thousand then six thousand uh to more  than twelve thousand can you confirm  that  well it is certainly the case that uh as  the cases have been made for more people  from afghan civil society to be  evacuated those names have been added um  in reality the focus is on the around  1800 eligible persons so uk passport  holders british nationals and the  remaining people under the arab scheme  which is about 2275  but there are  thousands more who  we would like to get out if there is  time and the capacity  when you say thousands more can you put  a figure on it slightly more specific  than that  the number changes all the time as  people are sort of brought to our  attention and the fact is we will get  out as many as we possibly can  but we've been clear throughout that  there is a hard reality that we won't be  able to get out everybody that we want  to and that's why it's very important  that we start to reassure people in  kabul because i know that people in  afghanistan are acutely aware of what is  being said in our media here in the uk  that the airlift is not the only route  out of afghanistan it is not the only  route to the uk there is a second phase  to this where people will be able to be  resettled in the uk  having been processed uh either at a  handling center in a refugee camp or  at one of our embassies or high  commissions in the region  if we have learned anything about this  government over the past 18 months it is  perhaps that on occasion perhaps more  than occasion you know policy decisions  taken at the very top happen at a  glacial pace there will be people who  will be pleased to hear the expansion of  the numbers under arab but you know the  former chief of defense staff lord  danette was talking about this at the  tail end of july i mean so too this this  conversation to take place with the u.s  president why is that happening tomorrow  why is that not happening today why did  it not happen over the weekend why did  it not happen well last monday when your  boss the defense secretary shed tears  when he conceded back then that not  everyone would be able to come out  because of the time frame on the  operation  well firstly i don't  accept that characterization of decision  making what i've seen over the last  10 days as this  operation pitting has been  going on has been an extraordinary pace  of decision making across government  decisions are being taken in in real  time to answer problems as they arise on  the ground and absolutely everybody here  in whitehall is focused on minimizing  the amount of policy friction  for our people doing a very difficult  job on the ground now  there is a wider question over  whether or not we knew that afghanistan  would fall at the pace that it did  i don't think we did i think our kind of  worst case assumption was that it would  take around six months to reach the  point that we're at right now and i  don't know that a lot earlier than it  became clear a lot earlier than tomorrow  that we would need to perhaps expand the  amount of time that we are on the ground  again i'll ask the question why has the  prime minister not spoken to the  president of the united states about  this beforehand it rather looks like the  u.s president isn't willing to take his  call is that the case  the foreign secretary has spoken to  secretary blinken yesterday the defense  secretary spoke to secretary austin so  we have been having these conversations  with the americans but just to be clear  i i think that everybody needs to  understand that this isn't just a  decision that gets taken in washington  the taliban get a vote on this as well  and i think it's far from certain that  the taliban are going to be willing to  allow the international community to  extend beyond the end of the month and  of course we have they're not willing we  know that they're not willing because  news of this uh lobbying of the us  president emerged we were able to put a  question directly to the taliban in doha  they have said that august the 31st is a  red line their red lines tend to be  written in blood  presumably the point at which we could  have approached the taliban about all of  this was you know a week and a half ago  where they were trying to work out where  the toilets were in the presidential  palace rather than know when they have  complete security control of kabul  i don't recognize that  this conversation could have been  happened happened a week or so ago  because i think nobody knew at that  point the sort of scale of the challenge  in fact  if you look at the number of people that  have been extracted in the last week  6631  that was pretty much the number that was  our target when this operation began so  what's happened in the meantime  is that people have come forward who  have an eligibility under our app  they've been added to the list and  that's why there are still about 2 000  of them to extract which we're working  on very hard to do and then beyond that  there has been an awful lot of people  from civil society that were not in our  plans to be clear they were not in our  plans there wasn't an eligibility they  weren't part of arab but the uk  government is trying to do the right  thing and i don't think we should  apologize for that but we have said we  will try to bring out as many as we can  now that poses a big military challenge  for the generals admirals air marshals  that are planning this mission but i  don't know that it's a bad thing to want  to stretch ourselves to bring out as  many people as possible rather than just  to have stuck with our original number  if no one is suggesting that it is a bad  thing minister what we are suggesting is  that we are we are lobbying the u.s  president to extend beyond august the  31st  after the taliban have said that they  will  they will respond and presumably  violently if that were to be the case i  mean  is our time in afghanistan solely  predicated on the americans if they  leave do we have to leave have we really  not investigated any other options  perhaps under nato for example about  remaining in that country if the us  decides once again to cut and run  well it is certainly the case that  the mission in kabul this week is  fundamentally underpinned by a u.s  presence not just in terms of the number  of troops that they have at the airport  assuring its security but also the role  that the u.s air force are playing in  delivering air traffic control and all  of the other airfield services so there  is a hard reality that there would be no  international airlift without the way  that the united states are are  underpinning it now  whether or not the us can be persuaded  to stay  is a matter for the prime minister  tomorrow in the g7 meeting  after the initial overtures made by both  the foreign secretary and the defense  secretary over the days previous but the  conversation with the taliban will then  follow and the taliban will have a  choice they can either seek to engage  with the international community  and show that they want to be  a  part of the international system they  want to be engaged in international  diplomacy or they can turn around and  say no there's no opportunity for an  extension so but i think everybody has  to be clear that this is not just the  discussion that happens amongst g7  leaders tomorrow it's a discussion that  then happens with the taliban if there's  the opportunity to stay we will but  right now our military planning is  focused on leaving on the 31st because  that's the deadline that is currently  written down uh as when everyone must be  out  it occasionally feels like the prime  minister's perhaps not not as well  informed about what is going on on the  ground as he perhaps should our  correspondent stuart ramsey has produced  some fantastic work in and around the  airport making clear the the dire scenes  that are facing many many thousands of  course the majority of whom will not  make it into the airport itself on  friday the prime minister said that  things at the airport were getting  better where if he'd switched on sky  news he would have been able to see that  they were not they were getting markedly  worse at that point did the prime  minister misspeak was he misinformed or  did he lie  no i also was on television on friday  saying that things were getting better  and they were in so much as  on the 24-hour period before friday we  got a thousand people out which was a  big acceleration on the number of people  that we got out  on any given on any other day that week  and in fact over the weekend we then got  another thousand out on satur on friday  and 1800 out  on  saturday and another 1800 out on sunday  so in that sense that shows that the  flow into the hc at the barren hotel was  improving now the problem was as  everybody saw  in the reporting coming out of kabul  that there was also  incredible proud challenges on saturday  that caused seven people to die  in the crash outside the barren hotel  and i've seen the reporting that  while around the wider airport there  were more people killed further  um that is horrendous our troops are  operating in the most awful  circumstances  what has changed over the weekend is  that the taliban have pushed the outer  corner further away from the baron hotel  which puts a bit more depth into the  process and also if i may now  they've also separated the us and the uk  queues  which makes a big difference to the  crush because a lot of the crush that  was outside the british gate were  actually people trying to access the us  system it's important to know as indeed  your excellent correspondent has done  that once those changes were made  the situation yesterday was much safer  and we're all very relieved about that  how has all of this affected affected  your view of our relationship with the  united states i know from speaking to  them that senior members of the cabinet  are livid  about the decisions taken by president  biden particularly the tone that he  struck in that that that very first  statement why is no one saying this  publicly you yourself served you will  share the view of many people that you  do not leave people behind you do not  cut and run  and that is what the u.s president has  demanded of us  well i mean you're absolutely right that  as an ex-soldier there's a sort of  deeply ingrained psyche that you never  leave a man behind and that's why we're  working every minute that we can to  maximize the number of people that we  that we bring out and there is a reality  to the fact that this airlift has to end  and that's why it's important to say  that there are other  routes to reach the uk in due course but  on the wider issue of uk us  relationships  no one's made any secrets of the fact  that we would have  preferred a more conditions-based  withdrawal that point was made at the  nato summit  but nato agreed collectively the  president is not wrong that we went in  together and we should all come out  together but from where i sit in the  ministry of defense i can tell you that  right now there is a royal navy ship of  haiti working for the united states  facilitating their relief effort after  the earthquake i can tell you that  there's a u.s warship and a u.s marine  corps air detachment under command of  the royal navy in the philippine sea  operating within our carrier strike  group the us and the uk are working  together in sub-saharan africa in the  north atlantic in eastern europe around  the world  the us and the uk remain the closest of  allies of course  when you disagree with your closest  friend it hurts it causes consternation  on both sides of the relationship but  absolutely nobody should think that the  us and the uk have anything but the  deepest and strongest of relationships  and there's stuff going on around the  world that shows that even if right now  in afghanistan there might be a little  bit of disagreement  minister good to have you in the program  this morning uh sorry for keeping you  quiet so long too appreciate it no  problem at all thank you

